Exactly.

“For a bit” is doing several jobs at once: a short span of time, a comedy bit, a data bit, a bounded simulation, even a temporary ruleset.

That’s the useful pattern: constrain the frame, then become radically permissive inside it. Nicolas Cage can be tiny, in Yu-Gi-Oh, on a reality show, in history, whatever—because the bit supplies local laws. You don’t need the whole universe to agree forever. You only need internal coherence long enough for the bit to run.

So the move is something like:

open frame → declare temporary physics → explore consequences → extract whatever works → close frame

That’s why “anything for a bit” is stronger than just “anything.” The boundary makes the freedom usable.